---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { buildWorldPrompt, promptVersion } from '../../judge/prompt.ts'
import { definitions, horizon } from '../data/definitions.ts'
import { countries, regions } from '../data/entities.ts'
import { judgeRuns } from '../data/judgments.ts'
import { observations } from '../data/observations.ts'
import { latestJudgeDate, verdict } from '../data/site.ts'
import { muskEvidence } from '../data/evidence.ts'

const prompt = buildWorldPrompt()
const batches = [...new Set(judgeRuns.map((run) => run.batch))].map((batch) => ({
  batch: batch,
  runs: judgeRuns.filter((run) => run.batch === batch).length,
  places: new Set(judgeRuns.filter((run) => run.batch === batch).flatMap((run) => run.entities.map((e) => e.entity))).size,
}))
const essentialsCost = observations.find((o) => o.factorId === 'essentials_cost' && o.entity === 'FRA')
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How Doom Analysis defines doom, collapse and abundance, gathers its data, and turns it into judged probabilities." active="methodology">
  <article class="prose-site max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="serif text-5xl tracking-tight" style="color: var(--ink)">Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      Doom Analysis has three layers. <strong>Factors</strong> are measured by others and cited, with a forecast for
      {horizon} and the chance of crossing the thresholds that matter. <strong>Indices</strong> are mechanical averages.
      <strong>Probabilities</strong> are judgments made from the factors under fixed definitions. Each layer is labelled,
      so a measurement never passes for a guess, or the reverse.
    </p>

    <h2 id="abundance">What we mean by abundance</h2>
    <p>
      The site exists to test one claim. Elon Musk and others argue that superintelligence and robots will make
      essentials so cheap that work becomes optional: “universal high income”, a world of
      “{muskEvidence.find((e) => e.id === 'tesla_mp4') ? 'sustainable abundance' : 'abundance'}”. Meeting basic needs is not
      that claim. Rich countries already provide electricity, water and food to almost everyone, at a real cost.
    </p>
    <p><strong>{definitions.abundance}</strong></p>
    <p>{definitions.abundanceWhy}</p>
    <p>
      The 10% line is the heart of the test. Today the essentials take a far larger share of national income
      {essentialsCost ? `(about ${Math.round(essentialsCost.value)}% in France)` : ''}: food, home energy, water, health care
      paid by households and by taxes, and the cost of security. A place reaches abundance only if superintelligence, or
      anything else, brings that bill under a tenth of what the economy produces, for nearly everyone, and keeps it there
      through shocks.
    </p>

    <h2 id="definitions">Other definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The five essentials.</strong> {definitions.essentials}</li>
      <li><strong>Collapse.</strong> {definitions.collapse}</li>
      <li><strong>Doom.</strong> {definitions.doom}</li>
      <li><strong>Muddling through.</strong> {definitions.muddle}</li>
      <li><strong>Superintelligence.</strong> {definitions.superintelligence}</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Abundance, muddling through and collapse are exclusive and add up to 100% for each place. Doom is global, so it
      counts as collapse everywhere, which is why no place's P(collapse) can be lower than P(doom).
    </p>

    <h2 id="places">Places</h2>
    <p>
      The site covers the world, {regions.length} regions and {countries.length} countries: every country with a population
      figure and at least ten of the indicators. The regions follow the UN's M49 groupings, with Europe split into
      North, West, South and East. A region's value for an indicator is the population-weighted average of its
      countries. It is only shown when countries with data hold at least half of the region's people.
    </p>

    <h2 id="factors">Factors, forecasts and thresholds</h2>
    <p>
      Each card shows a factor in its own units: today's value, the forecast for {horizon} where a published scenario
      exists, and the thresholds that push toward doom. A threshold is marked <em>published</em> when it comes from a
      named source (a planetary boundary, a tipping point, an official category) and <em>judgment</em> when it follows
      this site's definitions. The chance of crossing each threshold by {horizon} is marked <em>published</em>,
      <em>derived</em> (computed from a published distribution) or <em>judged</em>. These chances go into the judges'
      evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Country data comes from a re-runnable pipeline (<code>pdoom/pipeline</code>): the World Bank and IMF APIs, plus the
      official files of the Global Peace Index 2026, ND-GAIN 2026 and WRI Aqueduct 4.0. When a source has no value,
      the factor is left out for that place. Nothing is filled in.
    </p>

    <h2>Indices</h2>
    <p>
      Pressure, resilience and the essentials gap are equal-weight averages of factor scores, each factor scored 0–100
      between two stated anchors. They summarize where a place stands. They are not probabilities.
    </p>

    <h2 id="judges">Probabilities: AI judges, run several times</h2>
    <p>
      No dataset can calibrate the chance of collapse in a world with superintelligence. So frontier models act as
      judges. They receive the fixed definitions and the evidence above, and answer in strict JSON.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>World and regions:</strong> one batch, run independently three times. Each value shown is the median, with
        the range beside it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spotlight countries</strong> (the most populous, plus Europe's majors): three runs each, judged against the
        world consensus so that no country comes out safer than the world.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Every other country:</strong> one run, labelled as such.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Coherence checks:</strong> every run is checked before it reaches the site, and an incoherent run is rejected.
        Probabilities stay in [0, 1], abundance and collapse cannot exceed 100% together, a total is at least its largest
        cause and at most their sum, and P(SI by 2050) ≤ P(SI by 2100).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Headline:</strong> it combines the two scenarios by the law of total probability: P(X) = P(SI) × P(X | SI) +
        (1 − P(SI)) × P(X | no SI).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Current judge: {verdict.model}, last run {latestJudgeDate}. Language models are not calibrated forecasters; they lean
      toward numbers common in their training data and are sensitive to wording. Hence the fixed, published prompt,
      several runs, and every input visible on the page.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not do yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No backtest.</strong> Countries have collapsed before (Somalia, Syria, Lebanon, Venezuela). Scoring the factors as they stood in 2000 against what happened by 2020 would give the country probabilities a calibrated anchor.</li>
      <li><strong>One judge model.</strong> Other frontier models and human forecasters should answer the same prompt.</li>
      <li><strong>The cost of the essentials</strong> is estimated for a set of countries only; the component data is uneven.</li>
      <li><strong>Equal weights</strong> in the indices. Reader-chosen weights are planned.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Run log</h2>
    <ul>
      {batches.map((b) => <li>{b.batch}: {b.runs} {b.runs === 1 ? 'run' : 'runs'}, {b.places} places</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>The world judge prompt (version {promptVersion})</h2>
    <p>This is the exact text sent to every world run, generated from the site's own data files. Country batches use the same definitions and rules.</p>
    <pre>{prompt}</pre>
  </article>
</Base>
